WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.400
From jump scares to post-credit teasers,&nbsp;
movie fake-outs are everywhere. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.240
the most epic endings lull you&nbsp;
into a false sense of security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.680
right before the big finale. From classic&nbsp;
horror films to twisty crime tales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.880
these are the most epic endings that no&nbsp;
one saw coming. Prepare for spoilers.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.440
Based on the story by Stephen King, The Mist&nbsp;
has an ending so horrific that it's become one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:34.000
of the most notorious horror climaxes of all time.&nbsp;
King's original tale ended in a vague fashion: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.080
survivors of the story drive away with the hope of&nbsp;
being rescued, but death is still a possibility.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.160
“We gave it a good shot.&nbsp;
Nobody can say we didn’t.”

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:51.280
Frank Darabont's cinematic adaptation, however,&nbsp;
goes even darker. The car runs out of gas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.320
and the five survivors agree that they’d&nbsp;
rather not become a monster’s afternoon snack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.440
Because there are only four bullets left, the&nbsp;
main character shoots everyone — including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.280
his young son — and is left despairing,&nbsp;
traumatized, and ready to be devoured.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:13.120
It's an incredibly somber ending ... and&nbsp;
then he watches the military roll into town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.880
slaughter every creature in sight, and rescue&nbsp;
all the people they find on the way. If he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.400
could've waited just five more minutes,&nbsp;
his friends and son would still be alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.280
It's sick, completely unexpected,&nbsp;
and an utterly brilliant fake-out.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.280
Joe Wright's vastly underrated period&nbsp;
drama, Atonement, is one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.160
heart-wrenching portraits of class division&nbsp;
and wartime trauma ever committed to film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.920
For most of the movie, we follow the miserable&nbsp;
aftermath of upper-class Cecilia and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.920
working-class lover Robbie having their romance&nbsp;
interrupted by Cecilia's little sister Briony.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.560
She accuses him of a crime he didn't commit and&nbsp;
Robbie is sent off to war, but thankfully there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:05.680
a happy ending. He finally reunites with Cecilia,&nbsp;
and Briony agrees to confess about her lie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.560
and have the legal record changed. So&nbsp;
far, everything seems pretty cheery.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.200
“I’m sorry, could we stop for a moment?”

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.360
Except that the ending is a big stinking&nbsp;
lie. We see that the satisfying climax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.520
we've just watched was fabricated by an&nbsp;
elderly Briony, who is now a famous writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:32.560
hoping to rewrite history and atone for her&nbsp;
lies. In reality, Robbie died in Dunkirk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:39.040
and Cecilia died two months later in a tube&nbsp;
station bombing. And now we're all crying.

00:02:41.211 --> 00:02:44.560
Hot Fuzz pays loving tribute to&nbsp;
Hollywood action movie tropes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.440
so we probably should've&nbsp;
seen this fake ending coming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:54.240
Sargeant. Nicholas Angel uncovers the truth about&nbsp;
the Neighborhood Watch Association: the town&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.080
elders of Sandford are killing anyone who might&nbsp;
ruin their chances of winning Village of the Year.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.821
“The greater good.”

00:03:01.821 --> 00:03:02.880
“SHUT IT!”

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.280
With a little help from a violent swan, he brings&nbsp;
the operation leader into custody and everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.120
seems to be tidily wrapped up. While we're&nbsp;
back at the station for a cup of tea, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:21.920
the last remaining villain bursts in and shoots&nbsp;
poor Danny. But then, in the ensuing struggle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.240
he steps on a sea mine and blows&nbsp;
himself and the police station up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.640
For a moment there, we think that Danny died,&nbsp;
but the absolute final ending shows Danny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.880
surviving his gunshot wound and leading the&nbsp;
Sandford cops with the help of his BFF, Angel.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.800
The surprise endings of the endless Saw&nbsp;
sequels have become a bit of a cliché.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.240
But when the first one was released in 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.880
its fake ending genuinely caught people off guard&nbsp;
and provided a terrifying climax for horror fans.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.960
Initially, we're given an ending in which&nbsp;
the hospital worker Zep is revealed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:07.280
the serial killer all along. It doesn't make&nbsp;
a lot of sense, but the reveal does prompt one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:13.200
of the captives to saw off his own leg so&nbsp;
he can crawl out of his prison. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:20.720
Adam, the other captive, kills Zep with a&nbsp;
toilet tank lid. Good job, everyone! The end.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.800
“Good. Can I go home now?”

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.480
The movie's actual ending shows Adam&nbsp;
discovering that Zep was actually a victim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.920
Not only that, but the corpse&nbsp;
that's been lying in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.600
of the room the entire movie stands up&nbsp;
and reveals himself to be the real killer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:43.920
before abandoning a stunned Adam in the room&nbsp;
alone to die. Talk about a downbeat ending.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:51.440
Funny Games depicts a family whose vacation comes&nbsp;
to a screeching halt when two polite psychopaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:58.240
Peter and Paul, enter their lives to slowly&nbsp;
torture and kill them all ... which they do, very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:05.760
successfully. And by the end of the film, only&nbsp;
Ann the mom remains. In a natural and satisfying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.920
conclusion, Ann grabs a shotgun and kills&nbsp;
Peter with it, finally grabbing the upper hand.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.520
“You shouldn’t have done that, Ann.”

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.960
Except, this is a movie where the villains&nbsp;
have complete control over the narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:24.000
even breaking the fourth, winking at the camera,&nbsp;
as if the audience is an accomplice to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.640
crimes. With that in mind, Funny Games' actual&nbsp;
ending sees Paul using a TV remote to rewind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:37.840
scene itself, snatch the shotgun before Ann can&nbsp;
get it, and go on to enjoy the ending he wants.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.880
“That’s awesome, really. Really.”

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:47.040
The House of the Devil is a throwback film&nbsp;
set against the Satanic Panic of the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.800
and the characters have plenty of reason to panic.&nbsp;
The plot follows Samantha, a college student who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.080
accepts a job as a babysitter only to discover&nbsp;
that she's actually been hired to care for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:04.480
mysterious older woman. As she investigates the&nbsp;
creepy house, Samantha finds herself placed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:10.480
middle of a bizarre Satanic conception ritual,&nbsp;
courtesy of the creepy family who hired her.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:17.200
Samantha manages to escape and find herself a&nbsp;
gun, like an absolute boss. But upon realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.360
she may now be a vessel for something she'd rather&nbsp;
not be, she chooses to shoot herself in the head.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.600
In and of itself, that's a&nbsp;
gut-punch of an ending. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:34.080
in the actual final ending, Samantha survives&nbsp;
the gunshot wound ... and so does her unborn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.880
most-definitely-satanic child.&nbsp;
It's absolutely chilling.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.040
In 1979's Alien, Ellen Ripley&nbsp;
is having a really bad day at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.880
work. She just saw her workmates&nbsp;
get turned into a Happy Meal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:55.680
and even witnessed one co-worker give birth to&nbsp;
a monster via a ruptured chest cavity. So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.400
it's a relief when Ripley finally makes&nbsp;
it out of the whole thing in one piece.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.560
She escapes, climbs into a shuttle, and finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.120
lets her guard down. Unbunch those&nbsp;
shoulders, folks. It's a happy ending!

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.000
[screaming]

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.920
But in a delightfully cruel twist, there's&nbsp;
actually an alien stowaway on board Ripley's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:25.040
shuttle. Now that Ripley is relaxed and alone,&nbsp;
she's all the more vulnerable. Thankfully, Ripley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:31.200
also happens to be a one-woman, alien-killing&nbsp;
machine, and she manages to blast the toothy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:37.440
life form safely out of the craft so she can kick&nbsp;
back with her cat and live to see another day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:43.840
It'll take more than one measly alien and&nbsp;
one fake ending to defeat Ellen Ripley.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:50.800
Okay, so all the "Bohemian Rhapsody" headbanging&nbsp;
aside, Wayne's World remains the highest grossing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:56.320
movie based on a Saturday Night Live skit, and&nbsp;
also helped to usher in an era of wonderfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.680
self-aware cinema. Following a narrative&nbsp;
that pokes fun at how much of American media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.440
is designed to sell things to people, Wayne's&nbsp;
World provides a satisfying set of false endings.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.040
“It’s like people only do things because&nbsp;
they get paid. And that’s just really sad.”

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:19.840
First, there's a sad ending where everything that&nbsp;
can go wrong does go wrong, with Wayne smiling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.840
through a set of flames as his basement goes&nbsp;
up in smoke. Next up is the Scooby Doo ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.640
where Benjamin is revealed to be Old Man&nbsp;
Withers from the haunted amusement park.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.920
That seems like it might&nbsp;
bring the movie to a close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:39.280
but then Wayne and Garth opt to finish with&nbsp;
the "Mega Happy Ending," where everything is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:45.040
stupendous for absolutely everyone, and it's&nbsp;
air guitar solos and high fives all around.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:52.240
In a baffling example of movie studios thinking&nbsp;
that US audiences are too fragile, The Descent's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:58.160
fake ending can only be found in the original cut&nbsp;
of the movie, which was screened in the UK. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:03.840
a real shame because the OG ending features one&nbsp;
of the most brutal fake-outs in cinema history.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:08.000
After watching her entire friend&nbsp;
group get picked off by a set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.760
super ugly creatures, Sarah finally&nbsp;
escapes the cave, makes her way outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:19.440
and sees a hallucination of her recently&nbsp;
deceased friend as the screen fades to black.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.240
US audiences had the film finish&nbsp;
here to save their delicate souls.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.240
"I knew it. I knew this wasn’t right."

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:33.680
For UK viewers, however, the movie resumes with&nbsp;
the harrowing truth: Sarah is still in the cave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.360
She was simply hallucinating the entire escape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.360
Worse still, the crawlers can be heard&nbsp;
closing in on her as the screen cuts to black.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:47.600
It's dark, it's brutal, and it's&nbsp;
the only way to end the film.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.920
The fake ending of The Usual Suspects is probably&nbsp;
one of the most iconic in cinema history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:00.480
Back in a time when both director Bryan Singer and&nbsp;
star Kevin Spacey were well respected people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:06.320
their industry, the film dropped such a bombshell&nbsp;
that it was all anyone could talk about for years.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:12.080
After a suspenseful narrative, Detective&nbsp;
Kujan is sure that Dean Keaton is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:18.080
murderous crime lord Keyser Soze. Kujan&nbsp;
even gets a testimony from Verbal Kint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:24.400
a surviving witness with cerebral palsy. There's&nbsp;
a certain sense of finality to these scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:28.480
and that's exactly what makes the&nbsp;
results all the more surprising.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.000
“Oh. Gee. Thanks Dave. Bang-up job so far.”

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:37.920
We watch as Kujan slowly realizes that&nbsp;
Verbal fabricated his entire deposition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.360
And that's when we see the man we thought&nbsp;
was Verbal slowly losing his signature limp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.600
and flexing his supposedly disabled hand,&nbsp;
as the real Keyser Soze exits stage left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.200
and in plain sight. Not a single person saw&nbsp;
that ending coming at the time of release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.840
and we're still talking about&nbsp;
that crazy plot twist to this day.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:06.560
It's difficult to imagine the Snapchat generation&nbsp;
quaking in their boots over a cursed VHS tape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.520
but in 2002, it was still pretty spooky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.400
Even now, though, The Ring's fake&nbsp;
ending remains one of the scariest.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:20.480
The film draws to a nice, wholesome conclusion&nbsp;
with Rachel arranging a proper burial for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:26.400
a VHS well-dwelling corpse. The burial&nbsp;
appears to lift the curse from everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.440
but it turns out that the&nbsp;
corpse is a bit of a tease.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:31.040
“Why did you do that?”

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:37.760
Though Rachel has been spared, the curse still&nbsp;
exists for everyone else who's watched the tape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:40.320
including Rachel's dopey ex-boyfriend who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.480
watched it a week ago and is now&nbsp;
about to die a horrible death.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.720
Rachel races to save the poor guy, but by the&nbsp;
time she gets to him, his face is nothing but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:56.960
a deep fried mess. The real conclusion is where&nbsp;
Rachel realizes that she needs to copy the tape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:03.200
and pass it along to another unsuspecting fool&nbsp;
in order for her to save her son. And even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:09.840
we're way past the era of VHS tapes, the ending of&nbsp;
The Ring still makes us nervous to turn on the TV.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:15.360
“What about the person we show&nbsp;
it to? What happens to them?”

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:21.280
The 1976 adaptation of Stephen King's&nbsp;
first novel stunned and horrified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:26.560
audiences. After all, it's your classic&nbsp;
"girl-with-telekinetic-powers-meets-boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:30.640
girl-becomes-prom-queen,&nbsp;
girl-massacres-her-entire-class-before-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:33.680
killing-herself-and-her-abusive-mother"&nbsp;
high school romp.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:40.080
It's intense, to say the least. So when we&nbsp;
see the lone survivor, Carrie's friend Sue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:45.280
visiting her grave to pay some final respects,&nbsp;
it presents a much needed moment of calm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:52.320
Despite everything, Sue still feels remorse for&nbsp;
how poorly Carrie was treated when she was alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.640
and it looks like we're closing out&nbsp;
with a somber yet satisfying conclusion.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:04.720
Except, it's not the finale. Instead, in one final&nbsp;
scare, Carrie's hand launches out of her grave and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:10.720
attacks Sue in what's swiftly revealed to be&nbsp;
a dream sequence, teasing the idea of Carrie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:18.560
power beyond the grave. Modern audiences may be&nbsp;
overly familiar with such tricks, but in 1976, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:25.040
ending messed people up. It's one of the greatest&nbsp;
jump scares of all time, and Sissy Spacek even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:30.000
confessed that she used to go to theaters just&nbsp;
to watch the audience jump out of their chairs.

